  \def0{0} 
  \ifx#10\else^{0}\fi
_{w_{\alpha}}({\mathbb R}^d)$
are invariant under the semigroup
\[
P_t f(x) = \mathbb{E}[f(X_t(x))]
\]
corresponding to the Cauchy problem \eqref{eq:Cauchy}, and $P_t$ is a
quasi-contraction in each of these spaces: there is $c_\alpha>0$ such that
\begin{equation}
\|P_t f\|_{C 
  \def#1{0} 
  \def0{0} 
  \ifx#10\else^{0}\fi
_{w_{\alpha}}} \le e^{c_\alpha t}\|f\|_{C 
  \def#1{0} 
  \def0{0} 
  \ifx#10\else^{0}\fi
_{w_{\alpha}}}.
\end{equation}

Applying It\^o's formula to \(w_\alpha(X_t)\) and using Lemma~\ref{lemm:Lyapunov} gives \(P_tw_\alpha(x)\le w_\alpha(x)+c_\alpha\int_0^tP_sw_\alpha(x)\,ds\); Gronwall yields \(P_tw_\alpha\le e^{c_\alpha t}w_\alpha\), and positivity of \(P_t\) applied to \(|f|\le\|f\|_{C 
  \def#1{0} 
  \def0{0} 
  \ifx#10\else^{0}\fi
_{w_{\alpha}}} w_\alpha\) gives the stated estimate. Lemma~\ref{lemm:weighted_feller} gives the continuity of \(P_tf\), so \(P_tf\inC 
